From 10d69c6141824cfaf2e21f37edb421941bf1105d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Petr=20=C5=A0tetiar?= <petr.stetiar@prplfoundation.org>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 16:30:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] AP MLD: remove the borrowed link BSSes before deinitializing
 their driver

A non-first link BSS of an AP MLD has no driver context of its own.
hostapd_setup_bss() (src/ap/hostapd.c:1542-1546) and
hostapd_driver_init() (hostapd/main.c:175-180) copy hapd->drv_priv and
hapd->interface_added from the first BSS of the MLD
(hostapd_mld_get_first_bss()) and only add their own link to that
context. The context itself, an nl80211 i802_bss, was created by the
first BSS through hostapd_if_add() on the driver (struct
wpa_driver_nl80211_data) of the first BSS's interface. In the pWHM
configuration on prplOS every radio interface's bss[0] is a plain
non-MLD VAP (wlanN.1) and the AP MLD links are secondary BSSes, so the
MLD's first BSS and its partner links on the other radios all share an
i802_bss that hangs off one radio's driver.

When that radio's interface is removed, hostapd_interface_deinit()
takes the MLD BSS down and removes only its own link from the shared
i802_bss; the partner link keeps the context alive
(driver_nl80211_link_remove(), src/drivers/driver_nl80211.c:10965).
hostapd_cleanup_driver() (:3560) then decides what to do with the
driver by looking at bss[0] alone: a non-MLD bss[0] means
driver->hapd_deinit() unconditionally, and is_drv_shared() is never
consulted. wpa_driver_nl80211_deinit() (:3153) frees drv->rtnl_sk,
drv->first_bss and drv, and leaks every other i802_bss on the chain
with bss->drv pointing at freed memory. Nothing tells the partner BSSes
on the other interfaces: EVENT_MLD_INTERFACE_FREED is only sent when
the last link of a context is removed, so their hapd->drv_priv keeps
pointing at the leaked i802_bss and, through it, at the freed driver.

The next teardown of a partner then runs the whole nl80211 link
removal on that freed driver: send_and_recv_cmd() through drv->global,
rtnl_neigh_delete_fdb_entry() on the freed drv->rtnl_sk,
drv->ctx = bss->ctx written into the freed driver, and
wpa_driver_nl80211_if_remove() walking the freed first_bss chain.

Observed on prplOS with mac80211_hwsim (U40): pWHM's
SetRadioPowerDown() plus Radio.Enable=1 leaves netdevs wlan1.2 and
wlan1.3 in place (021), radio 1 re-adopts them ("Failed to create
interface wlan1.3: -23", continue using the existing netdev) and comes
up before radio 0, so the GUEST and backhaul MLDs' first BSSes live in
interface wlan1 and radio 0's links borrow their contexts. The
"disable all VAPs" step then sends REMOVE wlan1.1 followed one second
later by REMOVE wlan0.1:

  hostapd: Remove interface 'wlan1.1'
  hostapd: nl80211: deinit ifname=wlan1.1 disabled_11b_rates=0
  hostapd: Remove interface 'wlan0.1'
  hostapd: wlan1.3: AP-DISABLED
  hostapd: wlan1.3: CTRL-EVENT-TERMINATING
  kernel: hostapd[16247]: segfault at 8 ip ... in hostapd[...+0x4fa92]

A core of the reproduced crash (run-39b, pid 16026) has the backtrace

  error_handler+0x23 (arg == NULL)
  nl_wait_for_ack+71
  rtnl_neigh_delete_fdb_entry
  nl80211_remove_link
  driver_nl80211_link_remove
  hostapd_free_hapd_data
  hostapd_interface_deinit_free
  hostapd_remove_hapd_iface
  hostapd_global_ctrl_iface_receive
  eloop_run / main

i.e. the radio 0 partner of wlan1.3 removing its link through the
freed driver, with nl_wait_for_ack() running on the freed drv->rtnl_sk
and dispatching to a stale struct nl_cb (the heap still holds a freed
nl_cb with cb_err == error_handler). The instruction that faulted is
the libnl callback reading its NULL private argument; the exact write
that zeroed it was not identified, but every frame below it operates
on the freed driver.

Fix it in two steps:

1. Before an interface deinitializes its driver, remove the BSSes of
   the other interfaces that run on that driver.
   hostapd_remove_drv_borrowers() runs from
   hostapd_interface_deinit_free() and hostapd_disable_iface() ahead
   of the per-BSS teardown, and only for an interface whose bss[0] is
   not an AP MLD link, i.e. the case in which the driver is this
   interface's own and is freed with it.

   The borrowers are found through a new hapd->drv_owner_iface, the
   interface whose hostapd_driver_init() created the driver behind
   hapd->drv_priv. It is set at every hostapd site that assigns
   drv_priv (to the interface itself after hapd_init(), copied from the
   MLD's first BSS where a context is borrowed, copied from bss[0]
   where a secondary BSS is created through hostapd_if_add() on
   bss[0]'s driver or is pre-assigned bss[0]'s context), cleared at
   every site that drops drv_priv, and cleared in the BSSes of other
   interfaces when the interface is freed. The interface is a stable
   identity for the driver: it survives the removal of the MLD's first
   BSS and the replacement of the driver's first i802_bss, which the
   context pointer itself does not. It is NULL for a driver hostapd
   does not own (wpa_supplicant AP/mesh mode, the ucode pre-assignment
   in src/ap/ucode.c) and is compared, never dereferenced.

   A matching secondary BSS is removed with hostapd_remove_bss(), the
   same path as a global REMOVE of that BSS: its link is removed from
   the kernel and from the MLD while the driver is alive, AP-DISABLED
   and CTRL-EVENT-TERMINATING are reported, its control interface is
   closed, and its hostapd_data and configuration are freed. Keeping
   the BSS alive without a driver was considered and rejected: its
   control socket would stay reachable and several hostapd_drv_*()
   wrappers pass a NULL drv_priv straight into the driver. A bss[0]
   that runs on the departing driver takes its interface down with
   hostapd_disable_iface() instead, which ENABLE reverses. Once the
   partners are gone, the MLD's first BSS removes the last link of the
   context, the driver frees the i802_bss, and hapd_deinit() runs on a
   driver nobody else uses.

   The walk over interfaces->iface[] skips NULL slots: the shutdown
   loop in main() and hostapd_refresh_all_iface_beacons(), which the
   nested hostapd_disable_iface() reaches, both see slots that were
   already freed and set to NULL without count being decremented, so
   the latter gets the same guard.

2. Backport d0213ad1e443 ("AP MLD: Fix hostapd crash during interface
   deinit with non-ML BSS", Aditya Kumar Singh, 2025-03-05) into
   hostapd_cleanup_driver(): consult is_drv_shared() first, whether or
   not bss[0] is an AP MLD link, and only deinitialize an unshared
   driver. A shared driver behind a non-MLD bss[0] releases that BSS's
   context through hostapd_if_remove() instead, as upstream does. With
   step 1 in place this branch is a safety net for a context that step
   1 did not find. It is not a complete fallback: the driver it keeps
   alive is never freed on this tree and, once the last borrower link
   is removed, keeps a drv->ctx that points at a freed hostapd_data,
   so it trades a certain use-after-free for a latent one.

Behaviour change: partner links on other radios are removed, with
their configuration, when the radio hosting their driver context is
removed or disabled, instead of continuing on a driver that hostapd no
longer tracks. pWHM stops and starts hostapd per radio with global
REMOVE/ADD and re-adds every radio in its power-down and disable-all
flows, so the partner configuration is supplied again; a partner radio
that stays up needs a REMOVE/ADD of its interface to get the link back.

Not covered: an interface whose bss[0] is itself an AP MLD link keeps
upstream's semantics (the driver stays alive for the partners and is
leaked when the last partner link goes).

Signed-off-by: Petr Štetiar <petr.stetiar@prplfoundation.org>
---
